1
00:00:00,438 --> 00:00:02,188
- Previously on "The Ark"...

2
00:00:02,189 --> 00:00:03,481
- Tyson 5E.
- Whoever Maddox was meeting

3
00:00:03,482 --> 00:00:05,066
might still be there
waiting for her.

4
00:00:05,067 --> 00:00:06,276
- Come on over.

5
00:00:06,277 --> 00:00:07,861
- I'm not the man I was.

6
00:00:07,862 --> 00:00:10,155
- Something happened to Brice
as the shuttle took off.

7
00:00:10,156 --> 00:00:12,741
- Is the FTL charged?
- Yeah, but...

8
00:00:12,742 --> 00:00:14,784
- This ship is a safe haven
for clones.

9
00:00:14,785 --> 00:00:15,994
We can be whatever we want.

10
00:00:15,995 --> 00:00:18,121
- Fire EMP, Sasha.
- At what?

11
00:00:18,122 --> 00:00:19,622
- "Ark 22" has left.

12
00:00:19,623 --> 00:00:22,500
- Maddox was trying to help
Kurtis find a special G-44.

13
00:00:22,501 --> 00:00:25,503
- What's a G-44?
- You're looking at one.

14
00:00:25,504 --> 00:00:27,297
- [yells]

15
00:00:27,298 --> 00:00:29,300
- Let's get the hell
off this ship.

16
00:00:30,217 --> 00:00:33,178
[dramatic music]

17
00:00:33,179 --> 00:00:35,096
♪ ♪

18
00:00:35,097 --> 00:00:36,556
What the hell is going on?

19
00:00:36,557 --> 00:00:38,391
- I don't know,
but we need to get moving.

20
00:00:38,392 --> 00:00:41,519
- That doctor said something
about me being the one.

21
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:42,896
- Wait.

22
00:00:42,897 --> 00:00:44,439
Drake's wife said
he was searching

23
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:47,525
for a special G-44 clone,
but she didn't know why.

24
00:00:47,526 --> 00:00:50,695
- You talked to Drake's wife?
- Yeah, long story.

25
00:00:50,696 --> 00:00:53,531
Can we get out of here now?

26
00:00:53,532 --> 00:00:56,534
- There is an advantage
to looking like her.

27
00:00:56,535 --> 00:00:59,162
And, I mean,
you're about his size.

28
00:00:59,163 --> 00:01:00,580
- Drake to Morgan.

29
00:01:00,581 --> 00:01:02,666
What's the status
of the procedure?

30
00:01:02,667 --> 00:01:09,590
♪ ♪

31
00:01:12,218 --> 00:01:14,135
- [clears throat]
[comm beeps]

32
00:01:14,136 --> 00:01:15,637
We're about to begin, sir.

33
00:01:15,638 --> 00:01:17,597
- Good.
Let's not waste any time.

34
00:01:17,598 --> 00:01:20,100
My guards saw
that J6-82 from "Ark 1"

35
00:01:20,101 --> 00:01:21,309
running down the corridor.

36
00:01:21,310 --> 00:01:23,603
I'm sending you
two additional guards.

37
00:01:23,604 --> 00:01:24,979
- We have to go.

38
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:27,857
[uplifting music]

39
00:01:27,858 --> 00:01:34,782
♪ ♪

40
00:01:45,042 --> 00:01:46,459
- Okay, we'll go out
the back door,

41
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:48,503
straight to the shuttle,
and radio "Ark 1" from there.

42
00:01:48,504 --> 00:01:51,881
- Uh, Sharon, we're in FTL.

43
00:01:51,882 --> 00:01:53,466
- Shit.

44
00:01:53,467 --> 00:01:55,635
We can't detach a shuttle from
an Ark while traveling at FTL.

45
00:01:55,636 --> 00:01:58,221
It will break apart.
- Wait.

46
00:01:58,222 --> 00:02:01,475
I know a way,
but we need to get there first.

47
00:02:03,561 --> 00:02:06,313
[breathing heavily]
- Wait.

48
00:02:09,442 --> 00:02:10,817
Ian?

49
00:02:10,818 --> 00:02:13,778
[ominous music]

50
00:02:13,779 --> 00:02:17,115
♪ ♪

51
00:02:17,116 --> 00:02:18,533
This is--

52
00:02:18,534 --> 00:02:19,576
- Bad.

53
00:02:19,577 --> 00:02:21,494
This is bad, but we have to go.

54
00:02:21,495 --> 00:02:23,329
- What the hell
is Drake doing to them?

55
00:02:23,330 --> 00:02:26,834
- Nothing good.
- [breathing heavily]

56
00:02:30,296 --> 00:02:32,213
We have to do something.
- No, no, no.

57
00:02:32,214 --> 00:02:33,548
Sharon, these people
are clearly Gen 3s.

58
00:02:33,549 --> 00:02:34,674
They've already lost
their minds.

59
00:02:34,675 --> 00:02:35,800
Look at them.

60
00:02:35,801 --> 00:02:38,970
♪ ♪

61
00:02:38,971 --> 00:02:42,098
- But they're still people.

62
00:02:42,099 --> 00:02:43,350
We have to help them.

63
00:02:43,351 --> 00:02:46,186
- Team 2,
check sector 5, 7, and 13.

64
00:02:46,187 --> 00:02:47,645
- We have to go, now.

65
00:02:47,646 --> 00:02:48,855
- No. No.

66
00:02:48,856 --> 00:02:51,775
[dramatic music]

67
00:02:51,776 --> 00:02:53,777
♪ ♪

68
00:02:53,778 --> 00:02:56,696
- [scoffs] I thought "Ark 22"
were basing themselves here.

69
00:02:56,697 --> 00:02:58,573
I didn't think
that they'd leave.

70
00:02:58,574 --> 00:02:59,741
- Alicia.

71
00:02:59,742 --> 00:03:00,992
- There's just no way
of finding them now.

72
00:03:00,993 --> 00:03:05,789
We are so screwed!
- Hey! Take a breath, okay?

73
00:03:05,790 --> 00:03:08,750
- It's just--there's no way
of tracing Garnet or Ian's WD

74
00:03:08,751 --> 00:03:10,877
and no way to know
where the Ark went

75
00:03:10,878 --> 00:03:12,921
or even what direction
it was heading.

76
00:03:12,922 --> 00:03:15,006
- Hey, I once watched you
untangle this Ark

77
00:03:15,007 --> 00:03:16,675
from an alternate universe.

78
00:03:16,676 --> 00:03:18,551
You can figure this out.
- That was different.

79
00:03:18,552 --> 00:03:23,473
- No, you can science your way
out of anything, okay?

80
00:03:23,474 --> 00:03:26,267
- Hammond is requesting
a word with you.

81
00:03:26,268 --> 00:03:29,187
[tense music]

82
00:03:29,188 --> 00:03:34,984
♪ ♪

83
00:03:34,985 --> 00:03:38,029
- You've led us all
into grave danger.

84
00:03:38,030 --> 00:03:41,324
You disobeyed my direct orders.
- You're not my captain.

85
00:03:41,325 --> 00:03:43,201
- Well, your captain
left me in charge,

86
00:03:43,202 --> 00:03:44,786
and now you disobeyed us both

87
00:03:44,787 --> 00:03:47,706
and led the entire crew
into danger.

88
00:03:47,707 --> 00:03:50,959
Do you even have a plan?
- Yes, we do.

89
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:53,670
- I heard Drake's ship's gone.

90
00:03:53,671 --> 00:03:56,172
You have no idea
where they are, do you?

91
00:03:56,173 --> 00:04:02,220
♪ ♪

92
00:04:02,221 --> 00:04:05,682
You know, there may be
no more GSA,

93
00:04:05,683 --> 00:04:07,767
but I reached a rank of admiral

94
00:04:07,768 --> 00:04:11,229
through a lot of hard work
and experience.

95
00:04:11,230 --> 00:04:15,108
So tell me, Lieutenant,
have you ever commanded a ship

96
00:04:15,109 --> 00:04:17,819
while it was under attack?

97
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:19,946
Or launched an Ark
with 300 people

98
00:04:19,947 --> 00:04:22,407
while the Earth
descended into chaos?

99
00:04:22,408 --> 00:04:24,743
♪ ♪

100
00:04:24,744 --> 00:04:27,912
Well, despite what you
might think about me,

101
00:04:27,913 --> 00:04:29,914
I'm not
some power-hungry outsider

102
00:04:29,915 --> 00:04:32,334
looking for a command.

103
00:04:32,335 --> 00:04:33,753
I want to help.

104
00:04:35,338 --> 00:04:38,883
So let me out,
and we can fix this together.

105
00:04:40,593 --> 00:04:43,094
Or you can keep me in here,

106
00:04:43,095 --> 00:04:44,804
and we all die

107
00:04:44,805 --> 00:04:48,141
because of your
idiotic actions.

108
00:04:48,142 --> 00:04:50,894
- I'll take that
under advisement.

109
00:04:50,895 --> 00:04:57,818
♪ ♪

110
00:04:59,779 --> 00:05:02,072
[WD beeps]
- Lieutenant Reed to Drake.

111
00:05:02,073 --> 00:05:04,491
- Yes?
- We have a problem in Med Bay.

112
00:05:04,492 --> 00:05:07,452
[ominous music]

113
00:05:07,453 --> 00:05:13,083
♪ ♪

114
00:05:13,084 --> 00:05:14,834
- [sighs]

115
00:05:14,835 --> 00:05:19,047
♪ ♪

116
00:05:19,048 --> 00:05:23,343
Impose an immediate
ship-wide lockdown.

117
00:05:23,344 --> 00:05:27,473
And I want every J6-82
and G-44 identified.

118
00:05:28,516 --> 00:05:31,476
[alarm beeping]

119
00:05:31,477 --> 00:05:35,856
♪ ♪

120
00:05:39,318 --> 00:05:41,319
- We understand
the living quarters

121
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:43,154
are a bit tight right now,
but we're working--

122
00:05:43,155 --> 00:05:44,656
- Tight is an understatement.

123
00:05:44,657 --> 00:05:46,157
Ever since
Eastern Federation arrived,

124
00:05:46,158 --> 00:05:48,034
we've been sleeping in bunks.

125
00:05:48,035 --> 00:05:50,829
- We're working on expanding
our perimeters

126
00:05:50,830 --> 00:05:52,872
so we can build more housing.

127
00:05:52,873 --> 00:05:54,541
- We need more generators.

128
00:05:54,542 --> 00:05:56,418
Has anyone noticed that lights
have been going out

129
00:05:56,419 --> 00:05:59,421
in the EF quarters
but not in the GSA cabins?

130
00:05:59,422 --> 00:06:03,299
- There is no GSA
and no EF anymore.

131
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:05,552
We're all one here!
[crowd clamoring]

132
00:06:05,553 --> 00:06:08,847
- Hey!
Hey, quiet down, everyone!

133
00:06:08,848 --> 00:06:10,433
[door opens]

134
00:06:14,103 --> 00:06:17,147
- Welcome back, Mr. Strickland.

135
00:06:17,148 --> 00:06:19,774
I imagine it was a long night.

136
00:06:19,775 --> 00:06:21,609
- So it was.

137
00:06:21,610 --> 00:06:23,236
- Any news?
- No.

138
00:06:23,237 --> 00:06:26,740
No footprints, no belongings,
not a trace.

139
00:06:26,741 --> 00:06:29,075
But I haven't lost hope
of finding Angus or Kelly,

140
00:06:29,076 --> 00:06:30,994
and we should not give up.

141
00:06:30,995 --> 00:06:33,037
- Perhaps we should.

142
00:06:33,038 --> 00:06:35,081
I don't want to put others
in danger

143
00:06:35,082 --> 00:06:37,083
for such a futile cause.

144
00:06:37,084 --> 00:06:39,377
- It--it's not futile.

145
00:06:39,378 --> 00:06:42,339
- You just said you found
no sign of your friends.

146
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:44,466
- There's still a chance
they're alive.

147
00:06:44,467 --> 00:06:46,051
It's worth
the minimal danger to--

148
00:06:46,052 --> 00:06:47,677
- "Minimal danger"?

149
00:06:47,678 --> 00:06:49,763
We lost two people,

150
00:06:49,764 --> 00:06:52,807
vanished without a trace.

151
00:06:52,808 --> 00:06:54,684
And as someone reminded us,

152
00:06:54,685 --> 00:06:58,313
we all know the danger
that Kelly presents.

153
00:06:58,314 --> 00:07:01,232
- I will not accept
that scenario.

154
00:07:01,233 --> 00:07:03,943
We found no signs of foul play.

155
00:07:03,944 --> 00:07:06,613
[scoffs]
You know what?

156
00:07:06,614 --> 00:07:08,990
I'm going back out there
to search some more.

157
00:07:08,991 --> 00:07:10,450
- I advise you not to.

158
00:07:10,451 --> 00:07:12,077
- Who's with me?

159
00:07:12,078 --> 00:07:15,914
[soft tense music]

160
00:07:15,915 --> 00:07:18,166
[scoffs]
Anyone?

161
00:07:18,167 --> 00:07:21,753
♪ ♪

162
00:07:21,754 --> 00:07:22,921
- I'll go.

163
00:07:22,922 --> 00:07:30,096
♪ ♪

164
00:07:32,723 --> 00:07:35,558
- I think they're trying
to tell us something.

165
00:07:35,559 --> 00:07:38,645
- Who? Who is trying
to tell us something?

166
00:07:38,646 --> 00:07:40,272
- I don't know.

167
00:07:42,191 --> 00:07:44,317
- Could they be people
who worked for Mother?

168
00:07:44,318 --> 00:07:46,403
- Unless she invented
some new technology,

169
00:07:46,404 --> 00:07:48,196
I don't think they would have
been here long enough

170
00:07:48,197 --> 00:07:51,282
to build this.

171
00:07:51,283 --> 00:07:54,702
- Could another Ark have gotten
here even longer ago?

172
00:07:54,703 --> 00:07:55,954
- No, not much longer.

173
00:07:55,955 --> 00:07:58,124
All the Arks launched
within the same window.

174
00:08:01,085 --> 00:08:03,169
- So they're aliens.

175
00:08:03,170 --> 00:08:04,754
- They could be some sort

176
00:08:04,755 --> 00:08:07,674
of indigenous intelligent
creatures, I guess.

177
00:08:07,675 --> 00:08:08,967
Wouldn't that be cool?

178
00:08:08,968 --> 00:08:10,218
- "Cool"?

179
00:08:10,219 --> 00:08:12,262
They just tried
to dump us in a lava pit.

180
00:08:12,263 --> 00:08:14,389
- No, they showed us
a lava pit.

181
00:08:14,390 --> 00:08:17,892
That's why I think they're
trying to tell us something.

182
00:08:17,893 --> 00:08:21,438
- At this point, they're going
to starve us to death.

183
00:08:21,439 --> 00:08:24,107
If we don't get food soon,
we'll die.

184
00:08:24,108 --> 00:08:26,234
- Water.
- What?

185
00:08:26,235 --> 00:08:28,653
- If we don't get water soon,
we'll die.

186
00:08:28,654 --> 00:08:31,281
We can survive at least
four more days without water,

187
00:08:31,282 --> 00:08:34,534
and we can survive around
30 to 60 days without food.

188
00:08:34,535 --> 00:08:38,705
- You sound like you speak
from experience.

189
00:08:38,706 --> 00:08:41,249
- My family struggled a lot
back on Earth.

190
00:08:41,250 --> 00:08:44,294
[soft music]

191
00:08:44,295 --> 00:08:48,631
♪ ♪

192
00:08:48,632 --> 00:08:52,552
We sometimes went days
without food.

193
00:08:52,553 --> 00:08:54,846
- I'm sorry.

194
00:08:54,847 --> 00:08:56,598
- [sniffs]

195
00:08:56,599 --> 00:09:00,310
[scoffs] It's why I got
into horticulture.

196
00:09:00,311 --> 00:09:02,812
♪ ♪

197
00:09:02,813 --> 00:09:03,938
It felt comforting

198
00:09:03,939 --> 00:09:07,150
to know I could
make food anywhere...

199
00:09:07,151 --> 00:09:08,860
anytime.

200
00:09:08,861 --> 00:09:11,905
[rumbling]

201
00:09:11,906 --> 00:09:14,866
[tense music]

202
00:09:14,867 --> 00:09:21,874
♪ ♪

203
00:09:30,549 --> 00:09:33,885
- Why would they give us
a bunch of rocks?

204
00:09:33,886 --> 00:09:36,805
- Maybe they think
that's what we eat.

205
00:09:36,806 --> 00:09:43,728
♪ ♪

206
00:09:43,729 --> 00:09:46,940
[indistinct chatter]

207
00:09:46,941 --> 00:09:49,652
- Mmm.
How's your salad?

208
00:09:52,279 --> 00:09:54,155
Mine's great.

209
00:09:54,156 --> 00:09:56,324
I guess, uh...

210
00:09:56,325 --> 00:09:59,411
the sunshine
actually makes a difference.

211
00:09:59,412 --> 00:10:03,582
But, um, I'd die
for a steak right now.

212
00:10:07,586 --> 00:10:09,588
[sighs]

213
00:10:12,633 --> 00:10:13,926
Sanji...

214
00:10:15,594 --> 00:10:16,886
I'm sorry.

215
00:10:16,887 --> 00:10:19,597
Look, I don't--I don't know
what else to say.

216
00:10:19,598 --> 00:10:21,808
I know I overstepped
the other day--

217
00:10:21,809 --> 00:10:23,643
- "Overstepped"?

218
00:10:23,644 --> 00:10:26,980
I told you that I needed
to handle it myself,

219
00:10:26,981 --> 00:10:31,860
and then you immediately went
and threatened him.

220
00:10:31,861 --> 00:10:36,740
You don't understand
how it feels to go through--

221
00:10:36,741 --> 00:10:39,784
[somber music]

222
00:10:39,785 --> 00:10:45,915
♪ ♪

223
00:10:45,916 --> 00:10:47,959
- Did you know they were--

224
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:54,090
♪ ♪

225
00:10:54,091 --> 00:10:55,842
Sanji.

226
00:10:55,843 --> 00:10:58,636
Sanji, please, wait.
- I just--

227
00:10:58,637 --> 00:11:00,305
I really don't want
to get involved,

228
00:11:00,306 --> 00:11:03,516
but if Ves is
in a relationship with--

229
00:11:03,517 --> 00:11:05,810
she should know
about my past with him.

230
00:11:05,811 --> 00:11:07,312
- I agree.

231
00:11:07,313 --> 00:11:09,647
- But it's really none
of my business.

232
00:11:09,648 --> 00:11:12,150
- [sighs]

233
00:11:12,151 --> 00:11:14,402
You want me to talk to her?
- No!

234
00:11:14,403 --> 00:11:16,237
It's definitely not
your business.

235
00:11:16,238 --> 00:11:18,281
- [sighs]
Okay, look...

236
00:11:18,282 --> 00:11:19,949
[chuckles]

237
00:11:19,950 --> 00:11:22,619
I'm just trying to figure
this out, just like you.

238
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:25,080
- I--

239
00:11:25,081 --> 00:11:28,166
I have to do this
on my own, okay?

240
00:11:28,167 --> 00:11:30,168
- [sighs]

241
00:11:30,169 --> 00:11:32,337
♪ ♪

242
00:11:32,338 --> 00:11:35,257
[doors whir]

243
00:11:36,384 --> 00:11:38,718
- Thought I might
find you here.

244
00:11:38,719 --> 00:11:41,179
I brought you the new drink
from the bar.

245
00:11:41,180 --> 00:11:43,515
The Homebase Highball.

246
00:11:43,516 --> 00:11:45,683
- Really?
- Mm.

247
00:11:45,684 --> 00:11:47,519
A sweet and very strong drink

248
00:11:47,520 --> 00:11:48,812
is the last thing
they should be serving

249
00:11:48,813 --> 00:11:50,855
if they're trying
to ease tensions.

250
00:11:50,856 --> 00:11:55,193
But it might be just the thing
for someone who's feeling,

251
00:11:55,194 --> 00:11:57,320
I don't know,
a bit down in the dumps.

252
00:11:57,321 --> 00:12:01,908
- Thanks,
but I really need to focus.

253
00:12:01,909 --> 00:12:04,661
- Still concocting impossible
theories about Brice's death?

254
00:12:04,662 --> 00:12:06,788
- Come look at this.
[device beeps]

255
00:12:06,789 --> 00:12:08,790
I was able to zoom in
and enhance

256
00:12:08,791 --> 00:12:10,458
"Ark 1's" high-res hull cameras

257
00:12:10,459 --> 00:12:12,544
to see his death
from a different angle.

258
00:12:12,545 --> 00:12:14,212
- You really like putting
yourself through hell,

259
00:12:14,213 --> 00:12:15,380
don't you?

260
00:12:15,381 --> 00:12:16,840
- Look, his body
definitely disappeared.

261
00:12:16,841 --> 00:12:19,634
But if he was dragged away
by a creature, like you said,

262
00:12:19,635 --> 00:12:21,636
we would see drag marks,
and there aren't any, so--

263
00:12:21,637 --> 00:12:23,179
- The ground is hard.

264
00:12:23,180 --> 00:12:25,557
Drag marks wouldn't show,
or they've been blown away.

265
00:12:25,558 --> 00:12:27,642
- Or he walked away.

266
00:12:27,643 --> 00:12:29,936
- Eva, I know you're looking
for answers,

267
00:12:29,937 --> 00:12:33,106
but maybe it's time to admit
there just aren't any.

268
00:12:33,107 --> 00:12:35,150
You should spend your time
doing something

269
00:12:35,151 --> 00:12:37,402
that might actually result
in something good.

270
00:12:37,403 --> 00:12:39,362
[explosion]

271
00:12:39,363 --> 00:12:42,365
[tense music]

272
00:12:42,366 --> 00:12:49,498
♪ ♪

273
00:12:52,501 --> 00:12:55,754
[dramatic music]

274
00:12:55,755 --> 00:12:57,255
- It's been over two hours.

275
00:12:57,256 --> 00:12:59,215
Please tell me
you've made some progress.

276
00:12:59,216 --> 00:13:00,342
- I have.

277
00:13:00,343 --> 00:13:02,052
I've been analyzing
the spectral emissions

278
00:13:02,053 --> 00:13:04,095
from a trace amount of ionized
particles left behind

279
00:13:04,096 --> 00:13:06,639
by "Ark 22" to determine
which direction it went.

280
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:08,016
- And?

281
00:13:08,017 --> 00:13:10,685
- The results are inconclusive,
but I'm confident

282
00:13:10,686 --> 00:13:11,853
it will lead to something soon.

283
00:13:11,854 --> 00:13:14,230
- Okay, how soon?
- I don't know.

284
00:13:14,231 --> 00:13:16,649
- We can't keep the whole crew
stranded here forever.

285
00:13:16,650 --> 00:13:18,193
- No one said forever.

286
00:13:18,194 --> 00:13:20,153
- Well, then how long are you
gonna try to figure this out

287
00:13:20,154 --> 00:13:22,655
before we give up
and head back to Homebase?

288
00:13:22,656 --> 00:13:23,948
- "Give up"?

289
00:13:23,949 --> 00:13:27,160
We're not giving up.

290
00:13:27,161 --> 00:13:28,870
What did Hammond say to you?
- Nothing.

291
00:13:28,871 --> 00:13:30,705
It's just...

292
00:13:30,706 --> 00:13:32,457
we can only do this for so long

293
00:13:32,458 --> 00:13:35,293
before we have to move on,
Alicia.

294
00:13:35,294 --> 00:13:42,092
♪ ♪

295
00:13:42,093 --> 00:13:43,677
[alarm beeping]

296
00:13:43,678 --> 00:13:45,345
- Hey, Mike!

297
00:13:45,346 --> 00:13:48,014
You on for poker tonight?
- Oh, yeah. Sure am, buddy.

298
00:13:48,015 --> 00:13:49,641
Yeah.
- All right.

299
00:13:49,642 --> 00:13:51,602
- "Buddy"?
- I panicked.

300
00:13:55,689 --> 00:13:58,942
[device beeping, chimes]

301
00:13:58,943 --> 00:14:02,445
♪ ♪

302
00:14:02,446 --> 00:14:05,824
[device beeping, chimes]

303
00:14:05,825 --> 00:14:07,242
- Okay.

304
00:14:07,243 --> 00:14:08,618
We'll wait
for the guard to pass,

305
00:14:08,619 --> 00:14:10,829
and then we got a straight shot
to the shuttle.

306
00:14:10,830 --> 00:14:13,331
- Uh, Sharon?

307
00:14:13,332 --> 00:14:17,961
♪ ♪

308
00:14:17,962 --> 00:14:20,672
I guess they're making
new clones.

309
00:14:20,673 --> 00:14:26,970
♪ ♪

310
00:14:26,971 --> 00:14:28,555
- Coast is clear.

311
00:14:28,556 --> 00:14:29,640
Come on.

312
00:14:35,187 --> 00:14:37,856
- Could you give me more space?
- No, actually, I can't.

313
00:14:37,857 --> 00:14:40,859
- I'm working
as fast as I can. I--

314
00:14:40,860 --> 00:14:42,193
[beeps]
Oh, no.

315
00:14:42,194 --> 00:14:44,988
- What?
- I've been locked out. I--

316
00:14:44,989 --> 00:14:46,157
- What?

317
00:14:48,784 --> 00:14:51,453
[beeps]
What the hell? So have I.

318
00:14:51,454 --> 00:14:53,788
- Are the GSA override codes
still active?

319
00:14:53,789 --> 00:14:56,875
- I'm not sure.
- They sure are.

320
00:14:56,876 --> 00:14:59,544
All right, kids, fun's over.
- How did you get out?

321
00:14:59,545 --> 00:15:02,088
- [scoffs] I've been doing this
a long time.

322
00:15:02,089 --> 00:15:04,299
I'm a trained soldier.

323
00:15:04,300 --> 00:15:06,342
That guard you put on me
was not.

324
00:15:06,343 --> 00:15:07,927
He went down far too easy.

325
00:15:07,928 --> 00:15:09,262
- Please, Admiral,
we can't just leave--

326
00:15:09,263 --> 00:15:11,806
- I gave you a chance.

327
00:15:11,807 --> 00:15:14,059
Now we're doing things my way.

328
00:15:14,060 --> 00:15:16,644
♪ ♪

329
00:15:16,645 --> 00:15:17,854
- Hey!

330
00:15:17,855 --> 00:15:19,397
Where do you think
you're going?

331
00:15:19,398 --> 00:15:22,400
- Uh, we got an alert to check
on some noise near the hatch.

332
00:15:22,401 --> 00:15:24,194
- With the doctor?

333
00:15:24,195 --> 00:15:27,280
- We thought
someone might be hurt.

334
00:15:27,281 --> 00:15:29,032
- I'll scan you first.
- Hey.

335
00:15:29,033 --> 00:15:31,367
Uh, whoa, is that necessary?

336
00:15:31,368 --> 00:15:33,912
- Please remain where you are
and look into the device.

337
00:15:33,913 --> 00:15:36,581
- Oh, no need for that,
Mark, dear.

338
00:15:36,582 --> 00:15:37,582
They're with me.

339
00:15:37,583 --> 00:15:39,918
- Mrs. Drake,
we have strict orders

340
00:15:39,919 --> 00:15:43,088
to check all G-44s and J6-82s.

341
00:15:43,089 --> 00:15:44,506
- I understand that.

342
00:15:44,507 --> 00:15:46,591
But I'm telling you,
it's not necessary.

343
00:15:46,592 --> 00:15:48,134
- Come on.

344
00:15:48,135 --> 00:15:51,262
You're not really gonna dismiss
Kurtis Drake's wife, are you?

345
00:15:51,263 --> 00:15:53,181
Huh?
- [chuckles]

346
00:15:53,182 --> 00:15:55,683
Listen, I'll happily call
my husband,

347
00:15:55,684 --> 00:15:57,977
if that's what you want.

348
00:15:57,978 --> 00:16:00,980
Is that what you want?

349
00:16:00,981 --> 00:16:03,108
- No.
[clears throat]

350
00:16:03,109 --> 00:16:05,361
No, it's--it's fine.

351
00:16:07,780 --> 00:16:10,157
- Toodle-oo.
- [giggles]

352
00:16:12,952 --> 00:16:14,661
- Thanks for that.

353
00:16:14,662 --> 00:16:16,621
Uh, Clark, I presume.

354
00:16:16,622 --> 00:16:20,750
- Oh, just call me brother.
- [giggles]

355
00:16:20,751 --> 00:16:21,960
- I'm good.

356
00:16:21,961 --> 00:16:23,545
- Why are you helping us?

357
00:16:23,546 --> 00:16:25,922
- Because we want you
to take us with you,

358
00:16:25,923 --> 00:16:27,674
away from my husband.

359
00:16:27,675 --> 00:16:30,301
We're--we're in love,

360
00:16:30,302 --> 00:16:32,470
and we'd rather not keep
sneaking around.

361
00:16:32,471 --> 00:16:34,305
- Or get thrown
out the airlock,

362
00:16:34,306 --> 00:16:36,141
which will likely happen
if we stay here, so...

363
00:16:36,142 --> 00:16:38,059
- Well, taking you with us
is a risk.

364
00:16:38,060 --> 00:16:40,061
Why should we?

365
00:16:40,062 --> 00:16:43,440
- If you do, we'll tell you
everything you want to know,

366
00:16:43,441 --> 00:16:46,401
starting with why you should
really try some pink lipstick.

367
00:16:46,402 --> 00:16:48,653
It does wonders
for our complexion.

368
00:16:48,654 --> 00:16:50,780
[giggles]

369
00:16:50,781 --> 00:16:52,991
- Please, I know I can
figure out where they are.

370
00:16:52,992 --> 00:16:54,659
If you could just give me
more time, then--

371
00:16:54,660 --> 00:16:56,161
- You've had enough time.

372
00:16:56,162 --> 00:16:57,912
Frankly, I regret
not locking you out sooner.

373
00:16:57,913 --> 00:17:00,081
- But I know--
- Enough!

374
00:17:00,082 --> 00:17:01,499
I wanted you to see
for yourself

375
00:17:01,500 --> 00:17:02,876
how improbable your plan was,

376
00:17:02,877 --> 00:17:06,255
but the fact that you still
can't see it is alarming.

377
00:17:08,340 --> 00:17:11,843
Set a course for Homebase 1.
We're going home.

378
00:17:11,844 --> 00:17:18,642
♪ ♪

379
00:17:21,854 --> 00:17:24,272
Look...

380
00:17:24,273 --> 00:17:26,983
I am not making
this decision lightly,

381
00:17:26,984 --> 00:17:29,194
but I think you all know

382
00:17:29,195 --> 00:17:31,029
this is what Captain Garnet
would want.

383
00:17:31,030 --> 00:17:34,783
♪ ♪

384
00:17:34,784 --> 00:17:36,410
And she left me in charge.

385
00:17:38,370 --> 00:17:41,331
- I'll only take orders
from someone I trust.

386
00:17:41,332 --> 00:17:43,416
That would be Alicia or Garnet.

387
00:17:43,417 --> 00:17:45,335
- I feel the same way.

388
00:17:45,336 --> 00:17:47,671
- This is ridiculous.

389
00:17:47,672 --> 00:17:49,756
I'll do it myself.

390
00:17:49,757 --> 00:17:53,051
- Please, Admiral,
if you respect the GSA mores,

391
00:17:53,052 --> 00:17:55,553
then you know that we can't--
- I'm done arguing about this!

392
00:17:55,554 --> 00:17:57,055
- We won't take orders
from you!

393
00:17:57,056 --> 00:17:58,640
- Stand down!
- Wait, stop! Stop!

394
00:17:58,641 --> 00:18:01,726
I think we should listen
to Admiral Hammond.

395
00:18:01,727 --> 00:18:03,478
- What?

396
00:18:03,479 --> 00:18:06,523
- Look, I don't want to leave
our friends behind.

397
00:18:06,524 --> 00:18:10,568
You know I don't, but we don't
have a plan to find them,

398
00:18:10,569 --> 00:18:13,488
and even if you had all
the time in the world, Alicia,

399
00:18:13,489 --> 00:18:17,326
it's still a needle
in the giant haystack of space.

400
00:18:19,245 --> 00:18:22,789
We should go back to Homebase.

401
00:18:22,790 --> 00:18:25,917
I think it's what
Captain Garnet would do,

402
00:18:25,918 --> 00:18:27,377
don't you?

403
00:18:27,378 --> 00:18:30,588
[solemn music]

404
00:18:30,589 --> 00:18:34,926
♪ ♪

405
00:18:34,927 --> 00:18:36,970
- Yeah.

406
00:18:36,971 --> 00:18:38,763
Yeah.

407
00:18:38,764 --> 00:18:45,730
♪ ♪

408
00:18:47,857 --> 00:18:49,274
- What happened?

409
00:18:49,275 --> 00:18:51,526
Generators don't just explode
without reason.

410
00:18:51,527 --> 00:18:53,111
- Well, people are saying

411
00:18:53,112 --> 00:18:55,613
former EF engineers
illegally tried to reroute

412
00:18:55,614 --> 00:18:57,198
the electricity
to their quarters.

413
00:18:57,199 --> 00:18:59,576
They just blew up the generator
by accident.

414
00:18:59,577 --> 00:19:02,662
- Well, other people are saying
that former GSA engineers

415
00:19:02,663 --> 00:19:03,872
were doing the opposite.

416
00:19:03,873 --> 00:19:06,249
- What do we know?
- Not much.

417
00:19:06,250 --> 00:19:08,460
Just that this was
a targeted attack,

418
00:19:08,461 --> 00:19:10,920
which means
that it wasn't accidental.

419
00:19:10,921 --> 00:19:12,130
- How do you know?

420
00:19:12,131 --> 00:19:14,174
- I tested the residue
on the generator.

421
00:19:14,175 --> 00:19:16,634
It's made of an explosive
compound called trillium.

422
00:19:16,635 --> 00:19:18,762
- Trillium?

423
00:19:18,763 --> 00:19:21,222
Where would someone get access
to that?

424
00:19:21,223 --> 00:19:22,390
- That's just it--

425
00:19:22,391 --> 00:19:23,892
trillium is too volatile
to be carried on Arks.

426
00:19:23,893 --> 00:19:26,061
So we don't know
who brought it here or how.

427
00:19:26,062 --> 00:19:27,812
- What we do know
is that whoever did this

428
00:19:27,813 --> 00:19:30,398
wanted to create a large
and deadly explosion.

429
00:19:30,399 --> 00:19:31,900
[tablet chiming]

430
00:19:31,901 --> 00:19:34,319
- What do you know
about trillium...

431
00:19:34,320 --> 00:19:36,529
Mr. Avega?

432
00:19:36,530 --> 00:19:39,407
- "Mr. Avega."

433
00:19:39,408 --> 00:19:44,079
It's Captain,
and the answer is nothing.

434
00:19:44,080 --> 00:19:47,540
EF ships don't carry it
for the same reason as Arks.

435
00:19:47,541 --> 00:19:48,958
- Can you prove that?

436
00:19:48,959 --> 00:19:51,670
- What are you implying?
- Nothing.

437
00:19:51,671 --> 00:19:53,296
I'm just asking a question.

438
00:19:53,297 --> 00:19:55,965
- Well, I could ask you
the same question.

439
00:19:55,966 --> 00:19:58,843
Did someone from GSA bring it?

440
00:19:58,844 --> 00:20:02,764
Maybe this Maddox person
I keep hearing about?

441
00:20:02,765 --> 00:20:05,767
[dramatic music]

442
00:20:05,768 --> 00:20:10,898
♪ ♪

443
00:20:15,194 --> 00:20:16,861
- Dr. Kabir?

444
00:20:16,862 --> 00:20:17,987
- Hi, Ves.

445
00:20:17,988 --> 00:20:19,030
Uh...
[clears throat]

446
00:20:19,031 --> 00:20:21,533
May I call you Ves?

447
00:20:21,534 --> 00:20:23,910
- Well, that's what
everyone else is calling me.

448
00:20:23,911 --> 00:20:25,870
- [chuckles awkwardly]

449
00:20:25,871 --> 00:20:27,455
- How may I help you, Doctor?

450
00:20:27,456 --> 00:20:31,584
- Um, I was hoping that
we could speak in private.

451
00:20:31,585 --> 00:20:34,213
It's a personal matter.

452
00:20:36,090 --> 00:20:39,134
- So is this about Milan?

453
00:20:39,135 --> 00:20:42,053
- Uh, I'm--I'm not sure
what he told you, but--

454
00:20:42,054 --> 00:20:44,431
- That you used to be married.

455
00:20:44,432 --> 00:20:45,974
- Yes.

456
00:20:45,975 --> 00:20:50,478
- It sounds like it was
a difficult relationship.

457
00:20:50,479 --> 00:20:53,898
- Uh, that's one way
of putting it.

458
00:20:53,899 --> 00:20:55,692
[chuckles awkwardly]

459
00:20:55,693 --> 00:20:59,154
- Well, some people just don't
fit together, I suppose.

460
00:20:59,155 --> 00:21:02,699
- Yeah, well, um, this was
more than that.

461
00:21:02,700 --> 00:21:04,159
Um...

462
00:21:04,160 --> 00:21:08,288
Milan had a pretty severe
drinking problem

463
00:21:08,289 --> 00:21:10,832
and was abusive.

464
00:21:10,833 --> 00:21:14,753
- He quit drinking
when he came on "Ark 11."

465
00:21:14,754 --> 00:21:17,922
He's been sober ever since.

466
00:21:17,923 --> 00:21:21,051
- I'm glad to hear that.

467
00:21:21,052 --> 00:21:23,970
- I know Milan.

468
00:21:23,971 --> 00:21:28,058
I was on an Ark with him
for over two years.

469
00:21:28,059 --> 00:21:31,186
I guess that's at least...

470
00:21:31,187 --> 00:21:34,356
a year longer than you were
married to him, right?

471
00:21:34,357 --> 00:21:37,192
♪ ♪

472
00:21:37,193 --> 00:21:41,196
- Um, did he ever tell you
how we met?

473
00:21:41,197 --> 00:21:44,366
- He didn't tell me
much at all about you.

474
00:21:44,367 --> 00:21:46,367
- Oh.

475
00:21:46,368 --> 00:21:51,664
♪ ♪

476
00:21:51,665 --> 00:21:54,876
I was sitting on a bus...

477
00:21:54,877 --> 00:21:58,254
heading home from a late shift
at the hospital.

478
00:21:58,255 --> 00:22:01,716
He came over
and sat next to me,

479
00:22:01,717 --> 00:22:06,054
struck up a conversation,
and within two minutes,

480
00:22:06,055 --> 00:22:09,558
he said he knew
we would get married.

481
00:22:11,352 --> 00:22:13,520
And I knew...

482
00:22:13,521 --> 00:22:15,647
he was right.

483
00:22:15,648 --> 00:22:17,233
- Is there a point?

484
00:22:19,318 --> 00:22:20,652
- Yeah.

485
00:22:20,653 --> 00:22:23,780
The point is that...

486
00:22:23,781 --> 00:22:28,201
I know how charming he can be.

487
00:22:28,202 --> 00:22:31,663
You know when the last time
I saw Milan was?

488
00:22:31,664 --> 00:22:34,165
When he watched me drive away

489
00:22:34,166 --> 00:22:37,293
and he put his fist
through a wall.

490
00:22:37,294 --> 00:22:40,839
I applied for "Ark 1"
without telling him.

491
00:22:40,840 --> 00:22:43,049
When I got accepted,

492
00:22:43,050 --> 00:22:46,594
I left as fast as I could

493
00:22:46,595 --> 00:22:50,640
with whatever I could pack.

494
00:22:50,641 --> 00:22:54,644
I had to go to frickin' space
to get away from him.

495
00:22:54,645 --> 00:22:55,979
- Aw.

496
00:22:55,980 --> 00:22:57,814
- [scoffs]

497
00:22:57,815 --> 00:23:00,025
Okay.

498
00:23:00,026 --> 00:23:02,611
I just thought
you should know that.

499
00:23:04,530 --> 00:23:07,532
[breathing heavily]

500
00:23:07,533 --> 00:23:11,953
♪ ♪

501
00:23:11,954 --> 00:23:15,040
[chiming and beeping]

502
00:23:18,044 --> 00:23:20,420
- Well, Generator B is secure.

503
00:23:20,421 --> 00:23:22,589
The control panel
needed fixing,

504
00:23:22,590 --> 00:23:24,340
if you can believe that.

505
00:23:24,341 --> 00:23:27,635
You know, doing your work for
you is well below my pay grade,

506
00:23:27,636 --> 00:23:29,054
but I have to say,

507
00:23:29,055 --> 00:23:32,182
there is something thrilling
about going back to basics.

508
00:23:32,183 --> 00:23:35,435
It reminds me how brilliant
and generous I am.

509
00:23:35,436 --> 00:23:37,146
- That's nice.

510
00:23:39,357 --> 00:23:40,815
- So did you hear we voted

511
00:23:40,816 --> 00:23:42,901
and we're gonna turn Homebase
into a nudist colony?

512
00:23:42,902 --> 00:23:44,569
- Mm-hmm.

513
00:23:44,570 --> 00:23:45,695
Sounds good.

514
00:23:45,696 --> 00:23:47,113
- Well...
- Yeah.

515
00:23:47,114 --> 00:23:49,157
- Markovic!

516
00:23:49,158 --> 00:23:52,952
You haven't listened to a word
I've said, have you?

517
00:23:52,953 --> 00:23:55,872
- I'm sorry.
I'm just focused.

518
00:23:55,873 --> 00:23:57,500
- On what?

519
00:24:00,378 --> 00:24:03,797
- I triangulated
a repeating signal

520
00:24:03,798 --> 00:24:05,673
coming from Ross.

521
00:24:05,674 --> 00:24:07,217
It could be a transmission.

522
00:24:07,218 --> 00:24:09,260
- It's an automated
distress beacon.

523
00:24:09,261 --> 00:24:10,929
All Arks have them.

524
00:24:10,930 --> 00:24:12,847
That one's coming from "Ark 4."

525
00:24:12,848 --> 00:24:14,474
If you check back
on the SAV logs,

526
00:24:14,475 --> 00:24:16,017
you've probably been
getting it for days.

527
00:24:16,018 --> 00:24:17,477
- I did check the logs.

528
00:24:17,478 --> 00:24:20,772
It started
right after "Ark 1" left Ross.

529
00:24:20,773 --> 00:24:23,316
- Well, there could be
a dozen reasons for that,

530
00:24:23,317 --> 00:24:25,652
none of them to do
with human intervention.

531
00:24:25,653 --> 00:24:28,321
- Still, it's possible.

532
00:24:28,322 --> 00:24:32,075
- Have you done your work
on Generator C?

533
00:24:32,076 --> 00:24:33,536
- Not yet.

534
00:24:35,663 --> 00:24:37,288
- I'll do it.

535
00:24:37,289 --> 00:24:39,582
I'm just becoming
such a good person.

536
00:24:39,583 --> 00:24:41,668
- Yeah, good people
don't say stuff like that.

537
00:24:41,669 --> 00:24:44,380
- [scoffs]
So now you're listening to me.

538
00:24:45,923 --> 00:24:48,925
[jazzy music]

539
00:24:48,926 --> 00:24:53,972
♪ ♪

540
00:24:53,973 --> 00:24:57,058
[chuckles]
There you are.

541
00:24:57,059 --> 00:25:00,145
I've been looking
all over for you, Doctor.

542
00:25:00,146 --> 00:25:02,605
- What do you want, Mr. Trust?

543
00:25:02,606 --> 00:25:05,358
- Tell me, have you tried
the Homebase Highball?

544
00:25:05,359 --> 00:25:07,235
It's a delicious drink.

545
00:25:07,236 --> 00:25:09,237
- I'm an addict, remember?

546
00:25:09,238 --> 00:25:11,030
This is water.

547
00:25:11,031 --> 00:25:13,616
- I thought that was pills,
not spirits.

548
00:25:13,617 --> 00:25:16,161
- Well, I'm not about to trade
one addiction for another.

549
00:25:16,162 --> 00:25:18,705
- So why are you sat at a bar,
of all places?

550
00:25:18,706 --> 00:25:21,041
- I don't know, I always used
to go to the bar for a drink

551
00:25:21,042 --> 00:25:24,169
after a bad day on Earth.

552
00:25:24,170 --> 00:25:27,088
I guess old habits, you know?
- Mmm.

553
00:25:27,089 --> 00:25:30,383
Oh, you really need
to taste this.

554
00:25:30,384 --> 00:25:34,096
Uh, right.
Never mind.

555
00:25:35,848 --> 00:25:38,558
- Why were you looking for me?

556
00:25:38,559 --> 00:25:41,436
- Well, you know, I'm normally
completely oblivious

557
00:25:41,437 --> 00:25:43,104
to other people's emotions.

558
00:25:43,105 --> 00:25:46,483
Well, I believe--
- Don't worry. I'll be fine.

559
00:25:46,484 --> 00:25:48,985
- No, I'm talking about Eva.

560
00:25:48,986 --> 00:25:50,612
- Eva?
- Yes.

561
00:25:50,613 --> 00:25:52,739
Poor woman--
she spends all her hours

562
00:25:52,740 --> 00:25:54,240
watching and rewatching

563
00:25:54,241 --> 00:25:55,658
the video
of the Scotsman's death.

564
00:25:55,659 --> 00:25:58,453
- Yeah, I-I know it's been
really hard on her.

565
00:25:58,454 --> 00:26:00,246
- Yes, and I was hoping
that you could give her

566
00:26:00,247 --> 00:26:02,374
some kind of pill
to make it stop.

567
00:26:02,375 --> 00:26:04,667
She's getting
absolutely no work done.

568
00:26:04,668 --> 00:26:08,588
- [scoffs] You know,
there's no pill for grief.

569
00:26:08,589 --> 00:26:11,633
She's just gonna have to work
through it her way.

570
00:26:11,634 --> 00:26:13,968
It will take time.

571
00:26:13,969 --> 00:26:15,720
- How long?

572
00:26:15,721 --> 00:26:18,139
- You know,
I'm glad you care so much

573
00:26:18,140 --> 00:26:20,558
for Eva's well-being,
Mr. Trust.

574
00:26:20,559 --> 00:26:23,686
- No, this time
I'm talking about you.

575
00:26:23,687 --> 00:26:25,397
How long do you think
you're gonna be down

576
00:26:25,398 --> 00:26:26,773
in the proverbial dumps?

577
00:26:26,774 --> 00:26:28,733
- Uh, don't worry.
I'll be fine.

578
00:26:28,734 --> 00:26:30,819
- Okay, because, uh,

579
00:26:30,820 --> 00:26:34,614
you know, a lot of people
need you and...

580
00:26:34,615 --> 00:26:36,449
care for you.

581
00:26:36,450 --> 00:26:39,494
And I don't know
if you want to...

582
00:26:39,495 --> 00:26:42,205
talk about whatever that is
or something.

583
00:26:42,206 --> 00:26:45,041
- Um...

584
00:26:45,042 --> 00:26:47,585
[scoffs]
No, it--it--it's complicated.

585
00:26:47,586 --> 00:26:49,629
- Oh, well, I'm best
at complicated.

586
00:26:49,630 --> 00:26:50,755
[both laugh]

587
00:26:50,756 --> 00:26:53,091
But, actually,
that's, uh, more applied

588
00:26:53,092 --> 00:26:54,551
to physics than human emotion.

589
00:26:54,552 --> 00:26:58,596
But--but should you ever
need someone to talk to--

590
00:26:58,597 --> 00:27:00,223
- Thank you.

591
00:27:00,224 --> 00:27:03,226
♪ ♪

592
00:27:03,227 --> 00:27:06,271
[tense music]

593
00:27:06,272 --> 00:27:08,148
♪ ♪

594
00:27:08,149 --> 00:27:10,191
- Could it be a threat?

595
00:27:10,192 --> 00:27:13,403
Like, they'll break us
into little pieces

596
00:27:13,404 --> 00:27:14,863
if we don't behave?

597
00:27:14,864 --> 00:27:17,532
- [scoffs] That's a bit
extreme, don't you think?

598
00:27:17,533 --> 00:27:19,033
- No.

599
00:27:19,034 --> 00:27:20,660
They literally kidnapped us

600
00:27:20,661 --> 00:27:23,496
and imprisoned us
inside a cave.

601
00:27:23,497 --> 00:27:25,290
- I'm just saying,
maybe they're trying

602
00:27:25,291 --> 00:27:28,835
to say something more nuanced.

603
00:27:28,836 --> 00:27:30,712
Wait a second.

604
00:27:30,713 --> 00:27:33,631
♪ ♪

605
00:27:33,632 --> 00:27:35,842
Look at this.
Look, look!

606
00:27:35,843 --> 00:27:37,302
It's like some--

607
00:27:37,303 --> 00:27:40,221
it's like some kind of puzzle.

608
00:27:40,222 --> 00:27:41,681
There.

609
00:27:41,682 --> 00:27:44,100
Uh...

610
00:27:44,101 --> 00:27:47,270
there.

611
00:27:47,271 --> 00:27:49,981
[rumbling]

612
00:27:49,982 --> 00:27:54,569
♪ ♪

613
00:27:54,570 --> 00:27:55,738
Oh!

614
00:27:57,948 --> 00:27:59,532
Okay.

615
00:27:59,533 --> 00:28:02,619
That feels like progress.

616
00:28:02,620 --> 00:28:04,205
Okay.

617
00:28:05,915 --> 00:28:08,333
- Did you send a man
to secure the generator?

618
00:28:08,334 --> 00:28:09,668
- No, I didn't send anyone.

619
00:28:15,341 --> 00:28:16,883
- Then who's that, then?

620
00:28:16,884 --> 00:28:20,345
[ominous music]

621
00:28:20,346 --> 00:28:21,971
- Hey, you.

622
00:28:21,972 --> 00:28:24,224
- Uh, excuse me.
You're not supposed to be here.

623
00:28:24,225 --> 00:28:27,812
- Hey, hey, hey!
[both grunting]

624
00:28:28,396 --> 00:28:30,021
- Back away, Sheriff!
- What?

625
00:28:30,022 --> 00:28:32,107
- Back away!
Get out of here!

626
00:28:32,108 --> 00:28:35,236
[explosion]

627
00:28:38,114 --> 00:28:40,073
- All right, you said
you knew a way to drop

628
00:28:40,074 --> 00:28:41,282
this hunk of metal
out of an FTL bubble.

629
00:28:41,283 --> 00:28:42,784
Now's the time.
- Yeah, well, first off,

630
00:28:42,785 --> 00:28:43,952
let's not call her
a hunk of metal,

631
00:28:43,953 --> 00:28:44,994
'cause we're gonna want her
on our side.

632
00:28:44,995 --> 00:28:46,121
- What's the plan?

633
00:28:46,122 --> 00:28:47,247
- I'm gonna rig
our impulse coils

634
00:28:47,248 --> 00:28:48,957
to pull from the Ark
FTL retrofit

635
00:28:48,958 --> 00:28:50,333
and give us our own
temporary bubble.

636
00:28:50,334 --> 00:28:53,294
Just give me a minute.
- Wait, wait, wait.

637
00:28:53,295 --> 00:28:55,004
Is this something you knew
before the coma dream

638
00:28:55,005 --> 00:28:57,257
or something you learned
inside the dream?

639
00:28:57,258 --> 00:28:59,300
- We did it once on "Ark 8."
- And it worked?

640
00:28:59,301 --> 00:29:02,470
- Sort of.
- "Sort of"?

641
00:29:02,471 --> 00:29:05,724
- Well, aren't you two just
a good-looking little couple?

642
00:29:05,725 --> 00:29:07,308
- We are, aren't we?

643
00:29:07,309 --> 00:29:09,561
I'm sure you're aware,
but J6-82s are known

644
00:29:09,562 --> 00:29:11,229
for their animal magnetism.

645
00:29:11,230 --> 00:29:14,149
- We are, you know, which means
you got it, too, my friend.

646
00:29:14,150 --> 00:29:16,359
- [giggles]
- Good to know.

647
00:29:16,360 --> 00:29:18,528
How long you two been
together, then?

648
00:29:18,529 --> 00:29:20,655
- Oh, about one year,
six months.

649
00:29:20,656 --> 00:29:22,031
But who's counting?

650
00:29:22,032 --> 00:29:24,117
- I met Clark just when
I thought my life was over,

651
00:29:24,118 --> 00:29:25,702
when I found out
my husband was sleeping

652
00:29:25,703 --> 00:29:27,871
with three different G-44s.

653
00:29:27,872 --> 00:29:29,122
- Ouch.

654
00:29:29,123 --> 00:29:30,999
So he has a type, then,
does he?

655
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:32,834
- Can you blame him?
We're gorgeous.

656
00:29:32,835 --> 00:29:34,836
- Well, that goes
without saying.

657
00:29:34,837 --> 00:29:36,546
[both laugh]

658
00:29:36,547 --> 00:29:39,716
- Right, you two
better buckle in tight.

659
00:29:39,717 --> 00:29:41,302
It's gonna be a bumpy ride.

660
00:29:46,140 --> 00:29:48,099
Okay, drop us out of here.

661
00:29:48,100 --> 00:29:49,851
- Are you sure this will work?
- Not at all.

662
00:29:49,852 --> 00:29:51,061
- Ian!

663
00:29:51,062 --> 00:29:52,645
- Well, it probably will.
- "Probably"?

664
00:29:52,646 --> 00:29:54,272
- Yes, there's a good chance
it will work.

665
00:29:54,273 --> 00:29:57,942
But then again,
we might die, so...

666
00:29:57,943 --> 00:30:01,863
[soft music]

667
00:30:01,864 --> 00:30:04,240
- Wow, I--
- Sorry, I just--

668
00:30:04,241 --> 00:30:07,243
boundaries, I know,
but also maybe death, so...

669
00:30:07,244 --> 00:30:10,080
- Are you ready?
- Let's do this.

670
00:30:10,081 --> 00:30:15,543
♪ ♪

671
00:30:15,544 --> 00:30:17,337
[exhales sharply]
Okay.

672
00:30:17,338 --> 00:30:20,548
[breathing heavily]
- Hang on.

673
00:30:20,549 --> 00:30:22,801
Detaching from "Ark 22"

674
00:30:22,802 --> 00:30:25,720
in three, two, one.

675
00:30:25,721 --> 00:30:29,182
[exciting music]

676
00:30:29,183 --> 00:30:31,643
- [laughs]
- Hang on.

677
00:30:31,644 --> 00:30:35,480
♪ ♪

678
00:30:35,481 --> 00:30:38,024
- Come on.

679
00:30:38,025 --> 00:30:41,486
Come on! Come on!

680
00:30:41,487 --> 00:30:43,696
- [grunts]

681
00:30:43,697 --> 00:30:45,824
- [yelps]
- Whoa, whoa!

682
00:30:45,825 --> 00:30:48,493
[rumbling]

683
00:30:48,494 --> 00:30:50,245
- What was that?

684
00:30:50,246 --> 00:30:54,082
- It appears we lost FTL
bubble integrity for a moment,

685
00:30:54,083 --> 00:30:56,251
but it's been reestablished.
[alarm beeps]

686
00:30:56,252 --> 00:30:59,045
- Sir, uh, the "Ark 1" shuttle
is gone.

687
00:30:59,046 --> 00:31:02,007
[dramatic music]

688
00:31:02,008 --> 00:31:03,842
- Full stop...

689
00:31:03,843 --> 00:31:05,301
now.

690
00:31:05,302 --> 00:31:09,055
- Setting a course
for Homebase 1.

691
00:31:09,056 --> 00:31:11,016
[computer pings]

692
00:31:11,017 --> 00:31:12,684
- Wait.

693
00:31:12,685 --> 00:31:18,857
♪ ♪

694
00:31:18,858 --> 00:31:21,735
We just received a distress
ping from our shuttle.

695
00:31:21,736 --> 00:31:24,112
- That means Garnet
and Ian are alive!

696
00:31:24,113 --> 00:31:25,613
- Hope you're not trying
to pull something, crewman.

697
00:31:25,614 --> 00:31:28,158
- See for yourself.

698
00:31:28,159 --> 00:31:29,909
- Did a message come
with the signal?

699
00:31:29,910 --> 00:31:31,286
- It was just a ping,

700
00:31:31,287 --> 00:31:33,038
but it came on an encrypted
"Ark 1"-only channel.

701
00:31:33,039 --> 00:31:35,331
- It has to be them!
- Or it's a trap.

702
00:31:35,332 --> 00:31:37,334
- Nobody else
could have sent it.

703
00:31:37,335 --> 00:31:39,503
They'd need
Captain Garnet's code.

704
00:31:41,839 --> 00:31:43,423
- That was insane.

705
00:31:43,424 --> 00:31:45,759
- I can't believe
the bloody thing worked.

706
00:31:45,760 --> 00:31:48,428
[both laugh]

707
00:31:48,429 --> 00:31:51,181
- Now Alicia just has
to triangulate to the ping.

708
00:31:51,182 --> 00:31:52,932
Are you guys all right
back there?

709
00:31:52,933 --> 00:31:54,559
- We're fine.
- Wait, wait.

710
00:31:54,560 --> 00:31:56,728
What if "Ark 22" finds us
before Alicia does?

711
00:31:56,729 --> 00:31:59,147
- They won't. I sent the ping
under "Ark 1" encryption.

712
00:31:59,148 --> 00:32:00,899
"Ark 22" won't even see
the signal.

713
00:32:00,900 --> 00:32:03,109
[rumbling]

714
00:32:03,110 --> 00:32:05,320
- Are you sure about that?

715
00:32:05,321 --> 00:32:07,906
- How the hell did he find us?
- We're sitting ducks.

716
00:32:07,907 --> 00:32:10,075
♪ ♪

717
00:32:10,076 --> 00:32:11,743
- Drake won't fire on us.

718
00:32:11,744 --> 00:32:14,245
His doctor told me I'm the one.
He needs me for a reason.

719
00:32:14,246 --> 00:32:16,539
- Maybe,
but does he need you alive?

720
00:32:16,540 --> 00:32:20,418
♪ ♪

721
00:32:20,419 --> 00:32:21,586
[computer pings]

722
00:32:21,587 --> 00:32:24,422
- Yes.
[gasps]

723
00:32:24,423 --> 00:32:26,257
I did it.

724
00:32:26,258 --> 00:32:29,761
- You have the coordinates?
- I know where they are.

725
00:32:29,762 --> 00:32:32,013
♪ ♪

726
00:32:32,014 --> 00:32:34,891
All right.
[keys clacking]

727
00:32:34,892 --> 00:32:37,102
- There seemed to be
a misunderstanding.

728
00:32:37,103 --> 00:32:39,813
Why don't you come back to my
ship and we can talk it over?

729
00:32:39,814 --> 00:32:42,816
- How did you find us?
- You keep underestimating me.

730
00:32:42,817 --> 00:32:45,568
I simply traced where
the FTL disruption took place,

731
00:32:45,569 --> 00:32:47,612
and here you are.

732
00:32:47,613 --> 00:32:49,948
♪ ♪

733
00:32:49,949 --> 00:32:51,783
[rumbling]

734
00:32:51,784 --> 00:32:54,828
- Sharon.

735
00:32:54,829 --> 00:32:57,038
[exciting music]

736
00:32:57,039 --> 00:32:58,373
[both laugh]

737
00:32:58,374 --> 00:33:00,625
- Oh, they came back for you.

738
00:33:00,626 --> 00:33:03,169
How nice.

739
00:33:03,170 --> 00:33:04,587
Good people.

740
00:33:04,588 --> 00:33:07,424
[ominous music]

741
00:33:07,425 --> 00:33:10,552
Lock all weapons onto that Ark.

742
00:33:10,553 --> 00:33:12,596
- No.
No, no, no, no, no, no.

743
00:33:14,432 --> 00:33:15,932
- Whoa, whoa!
What are you doing?

744
00:33:15,933 --> 00:33:17,267
- The closer we get to "Ark 1,"

745
00:33:17,268 --> 00:33:18,435
the less likely
they'll fire on them.

746
00:33:18,436 --> 00:33:20,270
A stray blast
could kill us, too.

747
00:33:20,271 --> 00:33:23,022
He won't risk losing me
to destroy them.

748
00:33:23,023 --> 00:33:26,317
[dramatic music]

749
00:33:26,318 --> 00:33:29,237
- Ms. Nevins, fire EM pulse!

750
00:33:29,238 --> 00:33:31,489
What are you waiting for?
Fire before they fire on us!

751
00:33:31,490 --> 00:33:33,241
- The EM pulse
is not directional.

752
00:33:33,242 --> 00:33:36,661
It'll disable the shuttle, too.
- I knew this was a bad idea.

753
00:33:36,662 --> 00:33:37,787
Go to FTL!

754
00:33:37,788 --> 00:33:40,540
- No, Kimi, don't.
- Do it, Lieutenant Joma.

755
00:33:40,541 --> 00:33:42,167
- I think
with all your experience,

756
00:33:42,168 --> 00:33:45,295
you'd know it takes time
to recharge FTL, Admiral.

757
00:33:45,296 --> 00:33:52,344
♪ ♪

758
00:33:52,345 --> 00:33:53,970
- Can you get a clean shot
on "Ark 1"

759
00:33:53,971 --> 00:33:55,221
without hitting the shuttle?

760
00:33:55,222 --> 00:33:57,015
- Yes, from our
starboard cannon.

761
00:33:57,016 --> 00:33:58,892
- Perfect. Then fire.
- Really?

762
00:33:58,893 --> 00:34:00,852
They didn't do anything to us.
- Fire.

763
00:34:00,853 --> 00:34:03,272
♪ ♪

764
00:34:05,107 --> 00:34:07,817
[dramatic music]

765
00:34:07,818 --> 00:34:10,737
♪ ♪

766
00:34:10,738 --> 00:34:13,156
- [gasps, grunts]

767
00:34:13,157 --> 00:34:16,534
[victorious music]

768
00:34:16,535 --> 00:34:17,994
- Come on!

769
00:34:17,995 --> 00:34:19,829
[both laugh]

770
00:34:19,830 --> 00:34:26,961
♪ ♪

771
00:34:26,962 --> 00:34:29,089
- Well, don't look
so surprised.

772
00:34:29,090 --> 00:34:31,716
I scienced it.

773
00:34:31,717 --> 00:34:34,719
- Initiating
docking sequence now!

774
00:34:34,720 --> 00:34:37,847
♪ ♪

775
00:34:37,848 --> 00:34:39,224
- Oh, yes!

776
00:34:39,225 --> 00:34:40,892
The shuttle is docked
and locked.

777
00:34:40,893 --> 00:34:42,560
[chuckles]

778
00:34:42,561 --> 00:34:46,356
♪ ♪

779
00:34:46,357 --> 00:34:48,858
- They charged up
just in time, I guess.

780
00:34:48,859 --> 00:34:56,033
♪ ♪

781
00:34:58,869 --> 00:35:00,370
- Good work, Ms. Nevins.

782
00:35:00,371 --> 00:35:04,958
♪ ♪

783
00:35:04,959 --> 00:35:08,129
[both laugh]

784
00:35:11,841 --> 00:35:13,133
[both moan]
- I love you.

785
00:35:13,134 --> 00:35:14,175
- I love you.

786
00:35:14,176 --> 00:35:16,803
- You're my only.

787
00:35:16,804 --> 00:35:19,389
[both moaning]

788
00:35:19,390 --> 00:35:21,808
- You have to admit, they do
look kind of good together,

789
00:35:21,809 --> 00:35:23,768
don't they?
- [laughs]

790
00:35:23,769 --> 00:35:27,063
[both moaning]

791
00:35:27,064 --> 00:35:29,023
- Oh, Clark.

792
00:35:29,024 --> 00:35:31,359
- I just can't be without you.

793
00:35:31,360 --> 00:35:35,530
- [breathing heavily, giggles]

794
00:35:35,531 --> 00:35:36,614
- Two incoming!

795
00:35:36,615 --> 00:35:38,992
Sheriff Petrovic
and an unknown male.

796
00:35:38,993 --> 00:35:40,785
- "Unknown"?
- No ID.

797
00:35:40,786 --> 00:35:42,328
No one recognizes him,

798
00:35:42,329 --> 00:35:45,665
but they say he's the guy
who caused the explosions.

799
00:35:45,666 --> 00:35:48,418
- Um, I'll take John Doe.

800
00:35:48,419 --> 00:35:49,669
- Scanner.

801
00:35:49,670 --> 00:35:51,296
- Neuro scan.

802
00:35:51,297 --> 00:35:53,715
- I'm gonna need a CBC
and CMP on both.

803
00:35:53,716 --> 00:35:55,633
- And start IV fluids, stat.

804
00:35:55,634 --> 00:35:58,094
Who the hell is this guy?

805
00:35:58,095 --> 00:35:59,512
- Help. Dr. Kabir.

806
00:35:59,513 --> 00:36:02,182
I need medical attention
before I bleed out.

807
00:36:02,183 --> 00:36:04,517
- From the scratch
on your elbow?

808
00:36:04,518 --> 00:36:08,730
Give Mr. Trust a Band-Aid
and a lollipop.

809
00:36:08,731 --> 00:36:12,150
- Head injury here.
No other major trauma.

810
00:36:12,151 --> 00:36:13,360
- Yeah, I can't say
the same here.

811
00:36:13,361 --> 00:36:14,736
Hemoglobin is dropping,

812
00:36:14,737 --> 00:36:18,156
and he's got
extensive hemorrhaging.

813
00:36:18,157 --> 00:36:19,866
- Hold on, Dr. Kabir.

814
00:36:19,867 --> 00:36:21,701
I recognize these clothes.

815
00:36:21,702 --> 00:36:24,287
This is what Angus was wearing
the last time I saw him.

816
00:36:24,288 --> 00:36:26,956
- The T-shirt is inside out.

817
00:36:26,957 --> 00:36:30,543
♪ ♪

818
00:36:30,544 --> 00:36:32,253
What the hell?

819
00:36:32,254 --> 00:36:37,550
♪ ♪

820
00:36:37,551 --> 00:36:39,803
[keys clacking]
[knock at door]

821
00:36:39,804 --> 00:36:41,554
- It's open.

822
00:36:41,555 --> 00:36:43,556
- You wanted to see us,
Captain?

823
00:36:43,557 --> 00:36:47,852
- Yes, come in.
Close the door.

824
00:36:47,853 --> 00:36:50,397
[sighs]

825
00:36:50,398 --> 00:36:51,648
I thought you should know,

826
00:36:51,649 --> 00:36:53,566
Admiral Hammond told me
what you did.

827
00:36:53,567 --> 00:36:55,193
Actually, he came
to me to report

828
00:36:55,194 --> 00:36:56,945
he wasn't gonna press
court martial charges

829
00:36:56,946 --> 00:36:58,363
for what you did.

830
00:36:58,364 --> 00:36:59,614
- About that--
- It was my idea.

831
00:36:59,615 --> 00:37:01,449
- I told him,
since there is no GSA,

832
00:37:01,450 --> 00:37:03,451
there is
no court martial authority.

833
00:37:03,452 --> 00:37:05,161
But just the same,
I thought you should know

834
00:37:05,162 --> 00:37:06,579
he wouldn't have
pressed charges anyway.

835
00:37:06,580 --> 00:37:10,000
- Well, I guess that's good.
He doesn't hate us.

836
00:37:10,001 --> 00:37:11,543
- He would have been right
to charge us, though.

837
00:37:11,544 --> 00:37:13,878
- Damn straight he would have.

838
00:37:13,879 --> 00:37:17,215
You disregarded a direct order
by your commanding officer,

839
00:37:17,216 --> 00:37:19,092
one I left in charge
of this ship,

840
00:37:19,093 --> 00:37:21,469
so you also disregarded
my order.

841
00:37:21,470 --> 00:37:24,431
- Yes, ma'am, I'm prepared
to step aside from my duties.

842
00:37:24,432 --> 00:37:26,349
- That won't be necessary.

843
00:37:26,350 --> 00:37:30,729
The whole point of this
is to say I expect both of you

844
00:37:30,730 --> 00:37:33,273
to obey orders
while on this ship.

845
00:37:33,274 --> 00:37:35,067
- Yes, ma'am.
- I will.

846
00:37:36,652 --> 00:37:38,111
- Uh, is that all?

847
00:37:38,112 --> 00:37:40,071
- Yes.

848
00:37:40,072 --> 00:37:41,615
No, one more thing.

849
00:37:46,412 --> 00:37:50,081
Thank you for being stubborn,
insubordinate women

850
00:37:50,082 --> 00:37:52,417
who refuse to give up
on someone they care about.

851
00:37:52,418 --> 00:37:53,960
[soft music]

852
00:37:53,961 --> 00:37:55,295
It means a lot.

853
00:37:55,296 --> 00:37:57,088
- [chuckles]

854
00:37:57,089 --> 00:37:59,717
- Now get in here.
- [chuckles]

855
00:38:01,218 --> 00:38:02,553
- [chuckles]

856
00:38:04,805 --> 00:38:06,557
- Angus!

857
00:38:09,185 --> 00:38:11,729
Angus!

858
00:38:13,689 --> 00:38:16,274
Look, they kind of had
a point back there.

859
00:38:16,275 --> 00:38:18,276
How long do we keep searching?

860
00:38:18,277 --> 00:38:19,944
- Until we find them.

861
00:38:19,945 --> 00:38:22,614
- You must be exhausted.

862
00:38:22,615 --> 00:38:23,824
- I'm fine.

863
00:38:27,119 --> 00:38:29,245
- How's your daughter doing?

864
00:38:29,246 --> 00:38:31,623
- She's staying
with a friend from school--

865
00:38:31,624 --> 00:38:34,250
an endless sleepover,
as far as she's concerned.

866
00:38:34,251 --> 00:38:36,419
- Yeah, I remember those days,

867
00:38:36,420 --> 00:38:38,296
when any problem could be fixed
with a playdate

868
00:38:38,297 --> 00:38:39,798
and an ice cream cone.

869
00:38:39,799 --> 00:38:41,174
[chuckles]

870
00:38:41,175 --> 00:38:43,343
Just wait
till she's a teenager.

871
00:38:43,344 --> 00:38:46,471
- I try not to think
about that.

872
00:38:46,472 --> 00:38:49,516
- Yeah, nothing scarier
than a teenage girl...

873
00:38:49,517 --> 00:38:53,853
except a sociopath
with superhuman implants.

874
00:38:53,854 --> 00:38:55,480
Look, I know
you disagree, but--

875
00:38:55,481 --> 00:38:57,273
- Kelly did not kill
or hurt Angus.

876
00:38:57,274 --> 00:38:58,441
- You don't know that.

877
00:38:58,442 --> 00:38:59,692
- And you don't know
that she did.

878
00:38:59,693 --> 00:39:01,736
- Well, I know that you
don't have another theory

879
00:39:01,737 --> 00:39:03,655
about what happened.

880
00:39:03,656 --> 00:39:05,198
- [sighs]

881
00:39:05,199 --> 00:39:07,742
You're right, I have nothing...

882
00:39:07,743 --> 00:39:10,204
which only means
we're missing something.

883
00:39:12,331 --> 00:39:14,249
- Something like...

884
00:39:14,250 --> 00:39:16,042
this?

885
00:39:16,043 --> 00:39:18,962
[ominous music]

886
00:39:18,963 --> 00:39:21,131
♪ ♪

887
00:39:21,132 --> 00:39:23,633
- What the hell is that?

888
00:39:23,634 --> 00:39:30,641
♪ ♪

889
00:39:34,687 --> 00:39:36,646
- Oh, sorry.

890
00:39:36,647 --> 00:39:39,858
It's just--Ian, I've seen
a clone of you before,

891
00:39:39,859 --> 00:39:41,276
but not you, Sharon.

892
00:39:41,277 --> 00:39:44,237
- [chuckles]
It's bizarre, I know.

893
00:39:44,238 --> 00:39:46,781
- I mean,
you're just so identical.

894
00:39:46,782 --> 00:39:49,909
I mean, minus the hair
and the jewelry and the makeup.

895
00:39:49,910 --> 00:39:51,745
[laughs]

896
00:39:51,746 --> 00:39:54,372
- She could use some lipstick.
Trust me, I told her.

897
00:39:54,373 --> 00:39:55,790
[both laugh]

898
00:39:55,791 --> 00:39:57,459
- Lipstick or not,
she's beautiful

899
00:39:57,460 --> 00:39:59,169
'cause she looks like you.

900
00:39:59,170 --> 00:40:00,754
- [chuckles] See that?

901
00:40:00,755 --> 00:40:01,921
Always a charmer.

902
00:40:01,922 --> 00:40:03,882
Tell me, are you as madly
in love with yours

903
00:40:03,883 --> 00:40:06,259
as I am with mine?

904
00:40:06,260 --> 00:40:07,635
- Oh, we're--

905
00:40:07,636 --> 00:40:09,262
- Yeah. No, no, no,
we're not--we're not a thing.

906
00:40:09,263 --> 00:40:11,056
She's my boss.
- Yeah.

907
00:40:11,057 --> 00:40:14,684
- So what? I was his boss.
I mean, well, my husband was.

908
00:40:14,685 --> 00:40:16,227
- So you're in love
with this guy,

909
00:40:16,228 --> 00:40:17,771
but you're married to Drake?

910
00:40:17,772 --> 00:40:20,482
- Was. I...

911
00:40:20,483 --> 00:40:23,193
I prefer to think of,
was married to Drake.

912
00:40:23,194 --> 00:40:25,153
- So do you have any idea
what he's after?

913
00:40:25,154 --> 00:40:26,696
Why does he want these two?

914
00:40:26,697 --> 00:40:29,574
- And why was there a room
full of clones like you and me?

915
00:40:29,575 --> 00:40:32,327
- So many questions.
[laughs]

916
00:40:32,328 --> 00:40:34,913
[dramatic music]

917
00:40:34,914 --> 00:40:37,791
What it all comes down to
is this...

918
00:40:37,792 --> 00:40:41,002
my husband says you're the one.

919
00:40:41,003 --> 00:40:43,463
- That's what
the doctor said, too.

920
00:40:43,464 --> 00:40:46,132
- Hmm.
- What the hell does that mean?

921
00:40:46,133 --> 00:40:48,677
- It means you're the Gen 3
he's been searching for

922
00:40:48,678 --> 00:40:50,595
ever since we launched.

923
00:40:50,596 --> 00:40:51,971
- I don't know
what you're talking about.

924
00:40:51,972 --> 00:40:53,556
I'm not Gen 3.

925
00:40:53,557 --> 00:40:56,518
- Yes, darling, you are.

926
00:40:56,519 --> 00:41:03,651
♪ ♪

927
00:41:08,030 --> 00:41:10,949
- You let her get away.

928
00:41:10,950 --> 00:41:13,535
- They had shields.

929
00:41:13,536 --> 00:41:15,620
- You should have found a way
around them.

930
00:41:15,621 --> 00:41:18,289
- I tried, but--
- You tried?

931
00:41:18,290 --> 00:41:21,668
She's gone, you understand?

932
00:41:21,669 --> 00:41:25,088
And we don't even know
where Homebase 1 is.

933
00:41:25,089 --> 00:41:27,298
Evelyn would never tell me.

934
00:41:27,299 --> 00:41:30,218
You have to find Homebase 1.

935
00:41:30,219 --> 00:41:31,761
- How?

936
00:41:31,762 --> 00:41:33,888
No planet in the database
is called Homebase.

937
00:41:33,889 --> 00:41:36,016
- They obviously named it
themselves,

938
00:41:36,017 --> 00:41:38,977
so search all possible
habitable planets

939
00:41:38,978 --> 00:41:41,604
until you find it!

940
00:41:41,605 --> 00:41:44,983
- That could take a year or--
- I don't care!

941
00:41:44,984 --> 00:41:51,949
♪ ♪

942
00:41:53,284 --> 00:41:54,952
Find her.

943
00:42:26,859 --> 00:42:29,737
[thunder,
electricity crackling]

